  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Sir William Verdon-Smith was a British industrialist who chaired the Bristol Aeroplane Company and Bristol Tramways and Carriage Company. A nephew of transport pioneer Sir George White, he moved from running urban transport towards aircraft manufacturing. He left the tramways chairmanship to concentrate on aviation and was knighted in 1946.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 91,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Independent transport history and official honours establish the career summary."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Sir William Verdon-Smith was a British industrialist who chaired the Bristol Aeroplane Company and Bristol Tramways and Carriage Company. A nephew of transport pioneer Sir George White, he moved from running urban transport towards aircraft manufacturing. He left the tramways chairmanship to concentrate on aviation and was knighted in 1946.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "William Verdon-Smith began in Bristol's tramway business before joining the board of London United Tramways in 1894. He worked closely with James Clifton Robinson, the tramway engineer who chaired Imperial Tramways. He entered the industry during its transition from horse-drawn cars to electric trams, with Bristol opening its first electric route the following year.\n\nHis responsibilities also extended to civic and professional organisations. He chaired Bristol's Local Employment Committee and received a CBE in 1925. Later in that decade, he served as president of the company secretaries' professional body, now the Chartered Governance Institute. His presidency lasted for one year, with Colonel William Parker succeeding him.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 85,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Historical-society research, primary honours entry and full indexed institutional past-president list establish these distinct roles. No personal engineering invention or unverified civic outcome is attributed."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S5",
      "S7",
      "S8"
    ],
    "word_count": 106,
    "html": "<p>William Verdon-Smith began in Bristol's tramway business before joining the board of London United Tramways in 1894. He worked closely with James Clifton Robinson, the tramway engineer who chaired Imperial Tramways. He entered the industry during its transition from horse-drawn cars to electric trams, with Bristol opening its first electric route the following year.</p>\r\n<p>His responsibilities also extended to civic and professional organisations. He chaired Bristol's Local Employment Committee and received a CBE in 1925. Later in that decade, he served as president of the company secretaries' professional body, now the Chartered Governance Institute. His presidency lasted for one year, with Colonel William Parker succeeding him.</p>\r\n"
  },
